* {{alter|enm|markete|markett|markette|markyth|mercatt|merket|merketh|merkett||late}}

===Etymology===
From late {{inh|enm|ang|market||market}}, from {{der|enm|fro-nor|markiet}} ({{bor|enm|fro|marchié}}), {{der|enm|osx|markat}}, and/or {{der|enm|non|markaðr}}; all ultimately from {{der|enm|VL.|marcātus}}, from classical {{der|enm|la|mercātus||trade, market}}.

Variants ending {{IPAchar|/t/}} are from either Old Saxon or Old Picard, while those ending in {{IPAchar|/θ/}} are from other varieties of {{w|Old Northern French}} (e.g. [[Walloon]]).<ref>{{cite-journal|date=July 1992|author=Derek Britton|title=The ''-th'' spellings of English ''market''|journal=Neophilologus|volume=76|pages=446–451|doi=10.1007/bf00212154}}</ref><ref>{{cite-journal|date=July 1994|author=Christian Liebl|title=Some critical comments on V. Kniezsa’s <nowiki>‘</nowiki>''The post-Conquest lexical elements in the Peterborough Chronicle''<nowiki>’</nowiki>|journal=VIEWS: Vienna English Working Papers|volume=3|number=1|pages=35-44|publisher={{w|Universität Wien}}|url=https://archiv-anglistik.univie.ac.at/fileadmin/user_upload/dep_anglist/weitere_Uploads/Views/views941.pdf}}</ref>

